KW  - Die-sinking electrical discharge machining
KW  - Material removal rate
KW  - Surface roughness
KW  - Adaptive network-based fuzzy inference system
KW  - Grey relational analysis
AB  - Die-sinking Electrical Discharge Machining (EDM) is extensively utilized to machine intricate geometries in difficult-to-cut alloys, but optimizing competing parameters like Material Removal Rate (MRR) and Surface Roughness (SR) for Inconel 718 remains challenging due to strong nonlinear effects and process uncertainties. This study presents a hybrid framework that combines Taguchi-based Grey Relational Analysis (GRA) for multi-response optimization with an Adaptive Network-based Fuzzy Inference System (ANFIS) for forecast and interpretation. Experiments were conducted on Inconel 718 utilizing a copper tool in a kerosene dielectric based on an L27 orthogonal array. Five input variables, peak current, pulse-on time, pulse-off time, flushing pressure, and servo voltage, were investigated. Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) confirmed that pulse-on time and peak current were the most substantial factors influencing both output parameters. The optimized parameters enabled a higher MRR of 0.292 g/min and a lower SR of 1.9317 μm. GRA successfully balanced the trade-off between MRR and SR, with the best result observed at experimental input parameters (9 amps peak current, 25 μs pulse-on time, 20 μs pulse-off time, 0.6 bar flushing pressure, and 35 V servo voltage). The ANFIS model, validated using 5-fold cross-validation, exhibited strong predictive capability, with a coefficient of determination (R2) of 0.992 for MRR and 0.978 for SR, supported by low error metrics. The proposed Taguchi-GRA and ANFIS approach offers accurate prediction, reliable optimization, and practical guidance for enhancing efficiency and sustainability.
