KW  - Driver behavior modeling
KW  - Takagi-Sugeno fuzzy model
KW  - Shared steering control
KW  - Convolutional neural network
KW  - Robust control
AB  - Addressing the poor adaptability of assisted steering systems to driver characteristics and the unbalanced weight allocation, which potentially results in human-machine conflicts in human-machine collaborative steering, this paper proposes a collaborative steering control strategy based on driver preview characteristics, aiming to enhance the safety of human-machine collaborative steering. Initially, to address the limitations of traditional preview models in adapting to driver characteristics, a driver preview distance prediction system was constructed based on a convolutional neural network, which dynamically identified the driver's preview behavior characteristics through eye movement and head movement information. Subsequently, to effectively coordinate driver intention and vehicle states, a human-machine collaborative steering system was designed based on the Takagi-Sugeno (T-S) fuzzy model, which dynamically allocated the steering authority weighting factor based on fuzzy rules. Furthermore, to address uncertainties in preview time, weighting factors, and vehicle speed, a Takagi-Sugeno (T-S) fuzzy controller was proposed that optimized system transient response performance through pole placement, enhancing the disturbance rejection capability. Finally, to verify the effectiveness of the control, a Hardware-In-the-Loop (HIL) test platform was established. Comprehensive experimental results revealed that the designed controller significantly enhances performance across all tested scenarios, reducing driver workload by 46.0 % and lateral deviation by 84.4 % under normal conditions, and critically, ensuring robust vehicle stability under extreme maneuvers where driver-only control resulted in task failure. The research demonstrates that the proposed controller effectively alleviates human-machine conflicts and enhances path-tracking and collaborative driving performance, providing theoretical support and practical references for designing human-machine shared control systems in intelligent vehicles.
