AB  - You Only Look Once (YOLO) models are widely used for traffic monitoring via Closed-Circuit Television (CCTV) surveillance data. However, YOLO model often with pretrained weights perform poorly on low-resolution video feeds. To identify, track and extract information from low resolution surveillance traffic videos, we present a framework, TIE-LR (Traffic Information Extractor on Low-Resolution video), to combine multiple deep learning models to analyze vehicle's trajectories and traffic information from low-resolution CCTV traffic footage. The framework consists of four modules: 1) a distilled YOLO-based vehicle detection module; 2) SimCLR-based self-supervised learning to learn vehicles' representations for flexible vehicle classification; 3) ByteTrack-powered vehicle tracking module, and 4) trajectory processing module for aerial view conversion to extract vehicle trajectories. Specifically, SimCLR is used as a self-supervised to gradually learn vehicle's representation vectors to support vehicle classification without labeling thousands of images. Testing results show that vehicle identification results on 320 × 240 resolutions are comparable to the benchmark method using pretrained YOLOv8x model under 640 × 640 resolutions. Moreover, the proposed model gains a 17-fold speed gain compared to the benchmark method. By focuses on utilizing low resolution videos and use distilled model, TIE-LR improved the vehicle identification speed by over 17 times. Using low resolution videos, one can directly deploy the model on a wider range of established infrastructures (e.g., old cameras, limited data bandwidths, cheap computers). The TIE-LR framework can work as the foundation of a set of intelligent traffic control applications, including automated incident alerts, disabled vehicle detection, ramp metering optimization control, etc.
