KW  - Embodied intelligent robots
KW  - Large-scale multimodal models
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Survey
AB  - Despite the rapid development of traditional intelligent robots (TIRs) in last several decades and their widespread use in manufacturing, they are limited by rule-driven decision-making, and have issues dealing with complex dynamic environments and diverse task requirements. Embodied intelligent robots (EIRs) based on large-scale multimodal models (LMMs) show great potential to overcome these limitations, and over TIRs in terms of cross-modal information integration, semantic grounding in natural language interaction, generalization and adaptation in dynamic scenes, etc. Despite rapid progress in LMM-based EIRs, the field is still in its infancy, and systematic reviews on this topic are rare. To fill this gap and promote the development of the field, this survey provides a comprehensive review of research on EIRs from the perspective of LMMs. Firstly, the development process of EIRs is introduced, and then the briefs of LMM-based EIRs are provided. Secondly, we explore how LMMs enhance the artificial intelligence capabilities (perception, reasoning) and engineering application capabilities (execution, collaboration) of EIRs, respectively according to the practical core requirements of EIRs. Finally, the challenges and future development directions of LMM-based EIRs are discussed, and summarized.
