AB  - Cloth-changing person re-identification (CC-ReID) faces significant challenges due to clothing variations, which greatly impact model performance. Although recent multi-modal approaches have made notable progress, they still struggle to address clothing bias effectively. To overcome this limitation, a Multi-modal Text-enhanced Framework with Face-guided Query Enhancement Network (MT-FQENet) is proposed, it extends the Causal Textual Visual Network (CTVNet) by introducing a post-processing Face-guided Query Enhancement Module (FQEM) to further mitigate clothing bias during inference. Specifically, FQEM leverages facial cues to refine the matching between query and gallery by expanding high-confidence candidate sets and balancing the fusion between discriminative visual-textual and facial features. The textual modality provides clothing-independent semantic descriptions that complement visual and facial cues. Experimental results on three datasets demonstrate the superiority of MT-FQENet, and ablation studies further confirm the contribution of both text enhancement and the proposed FQEM module. This study provides new insights into developing robust and generalizable person re-identification models under appearance changes.
