AB  - Cooling windstorms, induced when cold waves cross mountainous terrain, pose significant risks to infrastructure and traffic safety. In this study, a wind speed prediction model is developed for such events. A seven-dimensional feature set is constructed to capture wind characteristics, followed by joint probability mapping and feature selection to enhance predictive performance. Eleven comparison models are used to evaluate different configurations within the proposed framework, and the optimal configuration is identified. Subsequently, a multi-step prediction study is conducted to evaluate 1- to 3-step predictions. The proposed model demonstrates superior performance, with Mean Absolute Percentage Errors of 19.61 %, 28.57 %, and 29.97 % for 1-, 2-, and 3-step predictions, respectively. These results highlight the robustness of the proposed model and its potential to enhance safety measures in mountainous regions during cooling windstorm events.
