KW  - Wave dynamics
AB  - Operational management of complex marine environments is often hindered by the failure of traditional forecasting models, leading to significant risks for important decisions such as vessel loading schedules and berth evacuations. In this paper, a paradigm shift is introduced from fallible prediction to robust diagnosis. A new, end-to-end causal diagnostic workflow is presented that uses systematic model failure as a tool for detecting unobserved confounding in such non-stationary systems. In this framework, physics-informed feature engineering, time-series causal discovery, and rigorous modeling experiments are integrated to identify directional pathways of influence. A consistent and reproducible predictive failure on unseen data has been observed, which is not interpreted as a limitation of the model, but as a powerful diagnostic confirming the presence of unobserved physical processes. It is noteworthy that formal sensitivity analyses demonstrate the exceptional robustness of the causal link between local wind speed and nearshore wave height despite this systemic unpredictability. Using this validated knowledge, a Causal Attribution Decision Support System (DSS) was developed, which was validated across 62 storm events. The results of this study provide a generalizable blueprint for building intelligent systems that provide actionable, real-time diagnostic insight. These results demonstrate that valuable insights can still be gained even when perfect prediction is not possible.
