KW  - Rate of penetration prediction DBSCAN
KW  - Hermite interpolation
KW  - Wavelet denoising
KW  - Ensemble learning
KW  - 1D-CNN
KW  - BiLSTM
AB  - Rate of Penetration (ROP) is an important measure of oil and gas well drilling efficiency. An accurate prediction on ROP is essential for optimizing drilling operations, achieving drilling automations and reducing drilling costs. However, real-time drilling data often suffers from outliers, high-frequency noise, and irregular sampling, which pose significant challenges to reliable ROP prediction modeling. To address these issues, this study proposes a Hybrid Data-Driven method for ROP prediction that integrates multi-stage data preprocessing framework with ensemble learning and deep neural networks. The data preprocessing framework combines Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise (DBSCAN) clustering for outlier removal, Hermite interpolation for reconstructing time series continuity, and wavelet transform with adaptive thresholding to effectively denoise the signal and enhance the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR). The refined data is first fed into a stacked ensemble model, which integrates Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) and Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM) as base learners, with a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) regressor serving as the meta-learner to aggregate their predictions. The ensemble outputs are then passed to a 1D Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN) for local pattern extraction, followed by a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) network to capture long-term temporal dependencies. Experimental evaluations on real-time drilling datasets demonstrate that the proposed method achieves high prediction accuracy with an R2 of 0.987. Comparative experiments against single-model baselines demonstrate that the proposed hybrid model achieves the R2 score improvement of approximately 9.32 %, reflecting its superior predictive performance and ability to capture complex relationships in drilling data.
ER  - 
